<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 3rd Grade (CEFR: A1.1)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> I Can Connect with Nature!</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I'm Outside. I'm Happy!</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Respond to basic questions about a familiar story. Interactive: Engage in a role-play based on a listened scenario.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can respond to basic questions about nature (e.g., "What can you hear outside?" "I can hear birds." "I can't hear the wind."). Can engage in a role play based on a story related to nature (e.g., "Now we will role play relaxing in nature."). Can respond to questions about a familiar story related to nature (e.g., "Where does the iguana go?").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-top: 30px; margin-bottom: 20px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 15%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification. Activate prior knowledge about nature and introduce key vocabulary receptively.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by playing a short audio clip of nature sounds (birds chirping, wind, rustling leaves, flowing water). Students close their eyes and listen. Then, the teacher shows large flashcards of a bird, a tree, a flower, the sun, and grass. The teacher asks, "What can you hear? What can you see?" encouraging students to point or give one-word answers if possible (e.g., "Bird!").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the pronunciation of target vocabulary while pointing to flashcards: "bird," "tree," "flower," "sun," "grass," "park." The teacher also models "I can hear..." and "I can see..." For pronunciation, the teacher explicitly models the /k/ sound in "can" and the /s/ sound in "sun," asking students to repeat. The teacher demonstrates blending /s/ /ʌ/ /n/ to form "sun" and /f/ /l/ /ɑʊ/ /ɚ/ to form "flower," slowly and clearly.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the words and phrases.
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing a bird flashcard) "Is this a bird or a tree?" (Students choose or say the word).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Making a listening gesture) "Can I hear with my eyes or my ears?" (Students point to ears).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to the sun) "Is the sun hot or cold?"</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "I Can Connect with Nature!" through a listening text.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A simple, short audio story (or teacher-read narrative) titled "A Day in the Park" featuring characters experiencing nature. The story uses controlled vocabulary (bird, tree, flower, sun, grass, park, listen, hear, see, happy, outside) and grammar (I can, I'm happy, I'm outside). Example story: "Lily is outside. She is in the park. She can hear birds singing. She can see a big green tree. She smells a beautiful flower. The sun is bright in the sky. Lily is happy."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Expressing connection with nature: Talking about sensory observations and experiences in nature (e.g., "I hear the birds." "The flowers smell nice.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question (First Listen):</b> The teacher plays the audio (or reads the story) once. Then asks, "Where is Lily? Is she inside or outside?" (Students indicate "outside" or point to an "outside" picture).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (Second Listen):</b> The teacher plays/reads again. Then states sentences and students give a thumbs up for True, thumbs down for False.
                        <ul style="list-style-type: circle; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 4px; margin-bottom: 0;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Lily can hear birds." (True)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Lily can see a red car." (False - she sees a tree)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Lily is happy." (True)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Matching (Third Listen):</b> Students listen again and match simple drawings (e.g., a bird, a tree, a flower) to words or phrases the teacher says (e.g., "hear birds," "see tree," "smell flower").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening, targeting specific language structures and vocabulary from the story.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> Can/Can't ("I can hear...", "I can't hear..."), Prepositions of place ("I'm outside."), Present simple ("I'm happy.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> bird, tree, flower, sun, grass, park, listen, hear, see, happy, outside.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> /k/ in "can", /s/ in "sun", blending for "sun" and "flower".</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Listening Gap-fill:</b> The teacher displays sentences from the story with missing words (e.g., "Lily can hear ____." "She can see a big green ____."). The teacher reads the original story again, pausing for students to listen and call out the missing word. Students can point to the correct flashcard or word on the board.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice - Listen and Identify:</b> The teacher shows a series of picture cards (e.g., a bird, a cat, a flower, a car). The teacher says, "I can hear a bird" or "I can't hear a cat." Students listen and raise their hand if they can hear the item, or shake their head if they can't. The teacher can then ask, "What can you hear outside?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction - Sounds in Nature:</b> Students work in pairs. The teacher provides each pair with a set of small nature picture cards (bird, stream, wind, flower, sun, tree). One student closes their eyes, and the other student makes a sound related to one of the cards (e.g., makes a "chirp" sound, or rustles paper for "wind"). The first student listens and says, "I can hear a [bird/wind]." They swap roles. The teacher circulates, checking for correct use of "I can hear" and pronunciation of /k/ and /s/.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">12-15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "I Can Connect with Nature!".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Role-play "Our Nature Spot."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in pairs. Each pair chooses a "nature spot" (e.g., a park, a forest, a riverbank) from a selection of large posters or drawn scenes. One student describes what they can hear and see in their chosen spot, and how they feel, while the other student listens and responds. They can use simple gestures or point to parts of their poster. Encourage them to use vocabulary like "tree," "bird," "flower," "stream," "grass," "sun," and phrases "I can hear...", "I can see...", "I'm happy," "It's peaceful."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Example Dialogue Snippets:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A: "I'm outside! I'm in the park. I can hear birds. They are singing. I can see a big tree."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B: (listening) "Oh, birds! That's nice. Can you see a flower?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A: "Yes, I can see a yellow flower. It smells nice. I'm happy!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B: "This is my favorite spot. It's peaceful."</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher monitors, listening for students' ability to respond to listened information and engage in simple interaction using target language. Encourage polite phrases like "Would you like to sit here?" (referring to a spot on the poster).</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment to check comprehension and production of target language.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> "Listen and Point/Draw." The teacher describes a simple scene in nature using familiar vocabulary and structures. Students listen and point to the correct picture from a set, or draw simple representations. Alternatively, the teacher asks individual students one-to-one questions about the role-play scenario (e.g., "What did you hear in the park?" "Can you see a tree?"). Students respond orally.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Observation):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Listening Comprehension:</b> Can respond to basic questions about the story/scenario (e.g., "Where does Lily go?" "What can you hear outside?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary:</b> Recognizes and uses 3-5 core nature words (e.g., bird, tree, flower, sun).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Grammar:</b> Attempts to use "I can hear..." or "I can see..." and "I'm happy/outside."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> /k/ in "can" and /s/ in "sun" are intelligible.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Actively listens and responds to partner during role-play.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a simple "I can" statement. "Today I learned to say: 'I can hear a ____.' I can hear [bird/wind/stream] (students draw or write one word). I am [happy/sad]."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Review and look ahead to future learning.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection Prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new nature words can you remember today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was your favorite sound to listen to?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The listening activities worked well to introduce new vocabulary and "I can hear/see." Students were engaged by the nature sounds and pictures.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Some students struggled with differentiating between 'can' and 'can't' without clear visual cues, requiring more modeling. The pronunciation of blended phonemes needs continued practice.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">For the next lesson (Reading Comprehension), I will need to provide more visual support alongside the text, ensuring the same core vocabulary is present and reinforced. I will also incorporate sentence frames for reading responses.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 2, students will read a short text or story that uses the same nature vocabulary (bird, tree, flower, sun, grass, park) and grammar (Can/Can't, I'm outside, I'm happy) introduced in this listening lesson, to develop their reading comprehension skills in context.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">3-5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>